        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe change is otherwise well-placed and coherent: the matching logic lives in `ClinicServiceImpl` alongside the sibling `existsOwnerWithIdentityKey`/`countOwnersByName` methods and reuses their exact `findAll().stream()` shape; the controller wiring mirrors the existing `namesakeCount` pattern (service returns the datum, controller sets it on the owner); the model, interface, schema-per-DB, and OpenAPI additions are all consistent and additive. No duplication, no catch-all growth.\n\nOne idiomatic finding:\n\n- `ClinicServiceImpl.findPossibleDuplicateOwnerId` (lines 347, 349, 350) uses fully-qualified `java.util.Objects.equals`, `java.util.Objects::nonNull`, and `java.util.Comparator.naturalOrder()` inline. This file's established convention is to import `java.util` types at the top (it already imports `java.util.Collection`, `List`, `Set`, `function.Supplier`), and the surrounding methods use simple names throughout. Add `import java.util.Objects;` and `import java.util.Comparator;` and reference them unqualified so the method reads like the rest of the file. A maintainer would find the inline FQNs jarring against the file's own style."
